window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"This document gives a general description of artificial intelligence (AI). It tells you: What AI is. How AI is different from a usual computer program. How an AI system learns. How a chat AI makes text. The limits and risks of AI. You do not need special knowledge to read this document."},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"what-ai-is","n":"2","t":"What AI is","x":"AI is a type of computer software. It does tasks that usually need a human mind. Examples of these tasks are: To identify a face in a photograph. To translate text from one language to a different language. To change speech into written text. To answer a question in a chat. To find a defect on a part from a camera image. A usual computer program obeys rules. A person writes these rules, one by one. An AI system is different. It finds its own rules. It finds these rules in a large quantity of examples. We call these examples data . Figure 1. In a usual program, a person writes the rules. In machine learning, the computer finds the rules in the data."},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"the-parts-of-ai","n":"3","t":"The parts of AI","x":"AI is a large area. It has smaller areas in it. Refer to Figure 2. Artificial intelligence is the full area. It includes all methods that make a computer do \"smart\" tasks. Machine learning (ML) is one part of AI. The computer learns from data. Deep learning is one part of ML. It uses a large neural network . Generative AI is one part of deep learning. It makes new text, images, sound, or code. A chat AI is an example. Each of these areas has its own module. Refer to Module 2, Machine learning , Module 3, Deep learning , and Module 4, Generative AI . Figure 2. Each area is a part of the larger area around it. Note All AI systems that you can use today are \"narrow\" AI. Each system can do only some types of tasks. A system that can do all human tasks does not exist at this time. We call that type of system AGI ."},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"how-an-ai-system-learns","n":"4","t":"How an AI system learns","x":"The learning procedure is training . The result of training is a model . The model keeps what it learned as a very large set of numbers. We call these numbers weights . Training is a loop. The loop has these steps. Refer to Figure 3. The model receives an example from the data. The model makes a guess. The system compares the guess with the correct answer. The system calculates the error. The error shows how far the guess is from the correct answer. The system adjusts the weights by a small quantity. This makes the next error smaller. The loop starts again with the next example. The loop can occur millions or billions of times. After many loops, the guesses become accurate. For more information about training, refer to Module 2, Machine learning . Figure 3. The training loop. Each loop makes the model a small quantity more accurate. Three types of learning Type What the system gets Example Supervised learning Data with the correct answers. Emails with the label \"spam\" or \"not spam\". Unsupervised learning Data with no answers. The system finds groups and patterns. Put customers into groups that buy the same products. Reinforcement learning A reward for a good action and a penalty for a bad action. A program learns to play a game. It gets points when it wins."},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"neural-networks","n":"5","t":"Neural networks","x":"Most modern AI uses a neural network. A neural network has many small units. We call these units neurons . The neurons are in layers. Refer to Figure 4. The input layer receives the data. For a photograph, each neuron receives the value of one pixel. The hidden layers do the calculations. The first layers find simple shapes, for example edges. The subsequent layers find larger shapes, for example eyes or ears. The output layer gives the result. For example: \"cat: 92%\". Each line between two neurons has a weight. A large weight makes the connection strong. A small weight makes it weak. Training adjusts all these weights. For more information about how a neural network calculates and learns, refer to Module 3, Deep learning . Figure 4. A neural network. Data goes from the left to the right. A real network can have billions of connections. Play video: But what is a neural network? (3Blue1Brown) Video 1. But what is a neural network? (3Blue1Brown, approx. 19 min) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"how-a-chat-ai-makes-text","n":"6","t":"How a chat AI makes text","x":"A chat AI uses a large language model (LLM). An LLM is trained on a very large quantity of text. This text comes from books, websites, and code. An LLM does one main task. It predicts the next word in a text. It does this task again and again. This makes full sentences and full answers. The procedure You write a question. We call this the prompt . The system cuts the text into small parts. We call these parts tokens . The model calculates a probability for each possible next token. The system selects one token. Usually it selects a token with a high probability. The system adds the token to the text. The system goes back to step 3. It stops at the end of the answer. Figure 5. The model gives a probability to each possible next token. The numbers in this figure are only an example. An LLM does not look up facts in a database. It makes text that is probable, from the patterns that it learned. This is very useful. But it is also the cause of some errors. Refer to Section 8. The transformer Most LLMs use a design that has the name transformer . A transformer uses a method with the name attention . Attention lets each word \"look at\" the other words in the text. Thus the model knows that \"bank\" in \"river bank\" is different from \"bank\" in \"bank account\". For more information about transformers, refer to Module 3, Section 8 . For more information about chat AI, refer to Module 4, Generative AI . Play video: Large Language Models explained briefly (3Blue1Brown) Video 2. Large language models explained briefly (3Blue1Brown, approx. 8 min) Open on YouTube Play video: But what is a GPT? Visual intro to transformers (3Blue1Brown) Video 3. But what is a GPT? Visual intro to transformers (3Blue1Brown, approx. 27 min) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"where-people-use-ai","n":"7","t":"Where people use AI","x":"Area Use of AI Phones Face unlock, voice assistants, and photo improvement. Health Help doctors find problems in X-ray and scan images. Transport Find the fastest route. Help a driver keep the car in the lane. Industry Find defects on parts. Predict when a machine needs maintenance. Office work Write drafts, make summaries, translate, and write code. Games and AR/VR Control characters, make content, and identify objects in a camera view."},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"limits-and-risks","n":"8","t":"Limits and risks","x":"Warning An AI system can give incorrect information that looks correct. Do not use AI output for medical, legal, financial, or safety decisions without a check by a qualified person. Know these limits of AI: Incorrect answers. An LLM can make facts, names, or references that do not exist. We call this a hallucination . Bias. A model learns from data that people made. If the data is not fair, the model can also be not fair. Old information. A model knows only the data from before its training stopped. Some systems can search the web to get new information. No real understanding. A model finds patterns. It does not have human experience of the world. Caution Do not give private data to an AI tool if you do not know how the tool keeps that data. Private data includes passwords, ID numbers, and company secrets. Procedure: use a chat AI safely Write a clear prompt. Tell the AI the task, the context, and the format of the answer. Read the answer carefully. Do a check of all important facts in a reliable source. If the answer is not correct, tell the AI the error and ask again. Make the final decision yourself."},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"summary","n":"9","t":"Summary","x":"AI is software that does tasks that usually need a human mind. Machine learning finds rules in data. A person does not write the rules. Training is a loop: guess, compare, adjust, repeat. A neural network has layers of neurons. Weights connect the neurons. A chat AI predicts the next token again and again. AI can make errors. Always do a check of important information."},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":2,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"This module gives a description of machine learning (ML). It tells you: The steps that make an ML system. The types of data that ML uses. The three types of learning. How a model finds its errors and makes them smaller. How to measure the quality of a model. Some common ML methods."},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":2,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"what-machine-learning-is","n":"2","t":"What machine learning is","x":"Machine learning is one part of AI. In ML, a computer learns from examples. A person does not write the rules. The computer finds the rules in the data. Think of a person who learns to identify ripe mangoes. Nobody gives that person a list of rules. The person looks at many mangoes. Each time, somebody tells the person \"ripe\" or \"not ripe\". After many examples, the person can identify a ripe mango alone. ML uses the same principle. Play video: A Gentle Introduction to Machine Learning (StatQuest) Video 1. A gentle introduction to machine learning (StatQuest) Open on YouTube | Find by title"},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":2,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"the-machine-learning-workflow","n":"3","t":"The machine learning workflow","x":"A team does these steps to make an ML system. Refer to Figure 1. Define the problem. Write what the system must predict. Collect data. Get many examples of the problem. Prepare the data. Remove errors and examples that occur two times. Divide the data into three sets. Refer to Section 4. Select a type of model. Train the model with the training data. Evaluate the model with data that it did not see before. Put the model into use. Monitor its results. If the model is not sufficiently accurate at step 7, adjust it and train it again. When new data becomes available, go back to step 2. Figure 1. The ML workflow. The dashed lines show the two loops back."},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":2,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"data","n":"4","t":"Data","x":"Features and labels ML data is usually a table. Each row is one example. Each column gives one item of information about the example. We call these columns features . The value that the model must predict is the label . Refer to Figure 2. Features: the inputs Label: the answer Area (m²) Bedrooms Floor Distance to station (km) Price (₹ lakh) 650 2 4 0.8 142 420 1 2 2.5 78 980 3 11 1.2 235 700 2 7 3.1 ? Figure 2. Data to predict the price of a flat. The blue columns are features. The green column is the label. The model must predict the \"?\" in the last row. All values are only examples. Warning Bad data makes a bad model. If the data has errors, gaps, or bias, the model learns them. Always do a check of the data before training. Three sets of data Before training, divide the data into three sets. Refer to Figure 3. Training set. The model learns from this set. Validation set. You use this set to adjust the model during development. Test set. You use this set one time, at the end. It shows the real accuracy of the model. Figure 3. A usual division of the data. The percentages can change for different projects. Caution Do not let the model see the test set during training. If it sees the test set, the test result will be too good. It will not show the real accuracy."},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":2,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"supervised-learning","n":"5","t":"Supervised learning","x":"In supervised learning, each example has a label. The model learns the relation between the features and the label. There are two main types. Refer to Figure 4. Regression predicts a number. Example: the price of a flat. Classification predicts a category. Example: \"spam\" or \"not spam\". Figure 4. Regression finds a line that predicts a number. Classification finds a boundary that divides the categories."},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":2,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"unsupervised-learning","n":"6","t":"Unsupervised learning","x":"In unsupervised learning, the examples do not have labels. The model finds patterns alone. A usual task is clustering . The model puts similar examples into groups. Refer to Figure 5. Example: a shop has data about its customers. Nobody knows the types of customer. The model finds three groups: students, families, and office workers. Then the shop can send different offers to each group. Figure 5. Clustering. The model finds the groups. It does not know the names of the groups. A person gives the names after."},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":2,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"reinforcement-learning","n":"7","t":"Reinforcement learning","x":"In reinforcement learning, an agent learns by trial and error. The agent is in an environment . Refer to Figure 6. The agent sees the current condition of the environment. We call this the state . The agent does an action. The environment changes. The agent gets a reward . A good action gives a high reward. A bad action gives a low reward. The agent changes its behavior to get more reward in the future. Robots, game programs, and some parts of chat AI training use this method. Figure 6. The reinforcement learning loop. The agent tries to get the maximum total reward."},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":2,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"how-a-model-makes-its-errors-smaller","n":"8","t":"How a model makes its errors smaller","x":"The loss During training, the system measures the errors of the model. The result is one number. We call this number the loss . A high loss shows many errors. A low loss shows few errors. The objective of training is to make the loss as low as possible. Gradient descent Most models use a method with the name gradient descent . Think of a person on a hill in fog. The person cannot see the bottom. But the person can feel the slope under the feet. The person takes a step downhill. Then the person feels the slope again and takes the next step. Gradient descent does the same thing with the loss. Refer to Figure 7. Calculate the loss for the current weights. Calculate the slope of the loss. This slope is the gradient . Move the weights a small step in the downhill direction. Do steps 1 to 3 again until the loss stops decreasing. The size of each step is the learning rate . Neural networks also use gradient descent. They use a method with the name backpropagation to calculate the gradient. Refer to Module 3, Section 4 . Figure 7. Gradient descent. Each green step moves downhill. The steps become smaller near the bottom because the slope becomes smaller. Caution Select the learning rate carefully. If it is too large, the model jumps over the bottom and does not become accurate. If it is too small, training is very slow. Play video: Gradient descent, how neural networks learn (3Blue1Brown) Video 2. Gradient descent, how neural networks learn (3Blue1Brown) Open on YouTube | Find by title"},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":2,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"overfitting-and-underfitting","n":"9","t":"Overfitting and underfitting","x":"A good model works correctly on new data. It does not only remember the training data. Refer to Figure 8. Underfitting . The model is too simple. It does not find the pattern. It makes errors on the training data and on new data. Good fit. The model finds the general pattern. It works correctly on new data. Overfitting . The model is too complex. It remembers each training example, also the random errors. It makes many errors on new data. Overfitting is like a student who memorizes the answers to old exam papers. That student cannot answer a new question. Figure 8. The same data with three different models. Only model B will give good predictions for new data. To prevent overfitting: Use more training data. Use a simpler model. Stop training when the validation loss starts to increase. Play video: Machine Learning Fundamentals: Bias and Variance (StatQuest) Video 3. Machine learning fundamentals: bias and variance (StatQuest) Open on YouTube | Find by title"},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":2,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"how-to-measure-a-model","n":"10","t":"How to measure a model","x":"After training, measure the model with the test set. For a classification model, use a confusion matrix . It compares the predictions with the correct answers. Refer to Figure 9. Figure 9. A confusion matrix for a spam filter. The filter examined 100 emails. Green cells are correct. Red cells are errors. Measurement Question that it answers When it is important Accuracy How many of all predictions are correct? When the categories have approximately the same size. Precision When the model says \"spam\", how frequently is it correct? When a false alarm is expensive. Example: a good email goes to the spam folder. Recall Of all the real spam, how much does the model find? When a missed item is dangerous. Example: a test for a disease. Note High accuracy is not always good. Think of a disease that occurs in 1 of 100 persons. A model that always says \"no disease\" is 99% accurate. But it finds zero sick persons. Thus, also look at precision and recall. Play video: Machine Learning Fundamentals: The Confusion Matrix (StatQuest) Video 4. Machine learning fundamentals: the confusion matrix (StatQuest) Open on YouTube | Find by title"},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":2,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"common-machine-learning-methods","n":"11","t":"Common machine learning methods","x":"There are many types of ML model. Each type is good for some tasks. This table shows some common types. Method Type How it works Example use Linear regression Supervised, regression Finds the straight line that is nearest to the data points. Predict the price of a flat. Logistic regression Supervised, classification Calculates the probability that an example is in a category. Predict if a customer will stop a subscription. Decision tree Supervised, both Asks a sequence of yes/no questions about the features. Approve or refuse a loan application. Random forest Supervised, both Uses many decision trees. Each tree gives a vote. Find fraud in card payments. k-nearest neighbors Supervised, both Looks at the most similar examples in the training data. Recommend similar products. k-means Unsupervised, clustering Puts the data into k groups around k center points. Put customers into groups. Neural network All types Many layers of connected neurons. Refer to Module 3, Deep learning . Images, speech, text, and chat AI."},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":2,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"summary","n":"12","t":"Summary","x":"In ML, the computer finds the rules in data. The ML workflow has eight steps, from the problem definition to monitoring. Features are the inputs. The label is the correct answer. Divide the data into training, validation, and test sets. Supervised learning uses labels. Unsupervised learning finds groups. Reinforcement learning uses rewards. Gradient descent makes the loss smaller, step by step. A good model works on new data. Prevent overfitting. Measure a model with accuracy, precision, and recall."},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"This module gives a description of deep learning. It tells you: What deep learning is, and how it is different from classic machine learning. How one neuron calculates its output. How a deep network learns with backpropagation. The main types of neural network. How a network finds patterns in images. How a network changes words into numbers. How a transformer uses attention. How to train a deep network in practice."},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"what-deep-learning-is","n":"2","t":"What deep learning is","x":"Deep learning is a part of machine learning. It uses neural networks with many layers. The word \"deep\" refers to the number of layers. A deep network can have from ten to more than one hundred layers. In classic machine learning, a person usually selects the features. For example, to identify a cat, a person can tell the model to measure the shape of the ears. In deep learning, the network finds the useful features in the raw data. Refer to Figure 1. Figure 1. In classic ML, a person selects the features. In deep learning, the layers of the network learn the features from the raw data. Deep learning is the best method for data that does not have a clear structure, for example images, sound, and text. Item Classic machine learning Deep learning Features A person selects them. The network finds them. Quantity of data Hundreds to thousands of examples. Thousands to billions of examples. Computer power A usual computer. Special chips (GPUs). Refer to Section 9. Explanation of results Usually easy. Difficult. Best for Tables of numbers. Images, sound, and text. Note For data in tables, classic methods are frequently as accurate as deep learning. They are also faster and easier to explain. Refer to Module 2, Section 11 ."},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"inside-a-neuron","n":"3","t":"Inside a neuron","x":"Module 1 tells you that a neural network has neurons in layers. Each neuron does a small calculation. Refer to Figure 2. The neuron receives numbers from the previous layer. It multiplies each number by its weight. It adds all the results. It adds one more number. We call this number the bias . It applies an activation function to the total. It sends the result to the neurons in the next layer. Figure 2. One neuron. It multiplies each input by its weight, adds the results and the bias, and applies the activation function. The numbers are only an example. The most common activation function is ReLU . ReLU changes negative numbers to zero. It does not change positive numbers. Note Without an activation function, many layers act the same as one layer. The activation function lets the network learn complex patterns, for example curves, and not only straight lines."},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"how-a-deep-network-learns","n":"4","t":"How a deep network learns","x":"A deep network learns with gradient descent. Refer to Module 2, Section 8 . But a deep network has millions or billions of weights. The system must find the gradient for each weight. A method with the name backpropagation does this quickly. Refer to Figure 3. The data goes through the layers, from the input to the output. The network makes a prediction. We call this the forward pass . The system compares the prediction with the label. It calculates the loss. The system goes back through the layers, from the output to the input. It calculates how much each weight caused the error. We call this the backward pass. The system changes each weight by a small quantity, in the direction that decreases the loss. The system does these steps again with the next group of examples. Figure 3. Backpropagation. The forward pass makes a prediction. The backward pass sends the error back through the layers. The system does not use all the training data at one time. It uses small groups of examples. We call each group a batch . One pass through all of the training data is an epoch . Training usually needs many epochs. Play video: Backpropagation, intuitively (3Blue1Brown) Video 1. Backpropagation, intuitively (3Blue1Brown) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"types-of-neural-network","n":"5","t":"Types of neural network","x":"There are different designs of neural network. Each design is good for a different type of data. Type Data How it works Example use Feedforward network Numbers in a table The data goes through the layers in one direction. Predict a price from features. CNN Images and video Small filters move across the image and find patterns. Refer to Section 6. Face ID. Find defects on parts. RNN Sequences, for example sound or sensor data It reads one item at a time. It keeps a memory of the previous items. Older speech and translation systems. Transformer Text, and now also images and sound Attention connects all parts of the input. Refer to Section 8. Chat AI, translation, and image generation. Transformers have replaced RNNs for most text tasks."},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"how-a-network-finds-patterns-in-images","n":"6","t":"How a network finds patterns in images","x":"A photograph has millions of pixels. A usual network would need too many weights. A convolutional neural network (CNN) solves this problem. A CNN uses small filters . A filter is a small grid of weights, for example 3 × 3. The filter moves across the image, one position at a time. At each position, it calculates one number. The result is a new grid that we call a feature map . The feature map shows where the filter found its pattern. Refer to Figure 4. Figure 4. A CNN. This filter finds vertical edges. The high numbers in the feature map show where the image changes from light to dark. The same filter is used at all positions. Thus a CNN needs fewer weights. It can also find a pattern in any part of the image. A CNN has many layers of filters: The first layers find edges and lines. The middle layers find shapes, for example circles and corners. The last layers find parts of objects, for example eyes or wheels. The output layer gives the result, for example \"cat: 92%\". Training finds the values in the filters. A person does not design them. Play video: Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) explained (deeplizard) Video 2. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) explained (deeplizard) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"how-a-network-changes-words-into-numbers","n":"7","t":"How a network changes words into numbers","x":"A neural network can use only numbers. Thus the system must change words, images, and other items into numbers. An embedding is a list of numbers that shows the meaning of an item. We call a list of numbers a vector . The network learns the embeddings during training. Items with similar meanings get similar numbers. Thus their embeddings are near each other. Refer to Figure 5. Figure 5. Embeddings. Words with similar meanings are near each other. A real embedding has hundreds or thousands of numbers. This figure shows only two numbers. Systems use embeddings to: Find text with the same meaning, not only the same words. Refer to Module 4, Section 8 . Recommend similar products, films, or songs. Put similar documents into groups. Start the calculation in a transformer. Refer to Section 8."},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"how-a-transformer-uses-attention","n":"8","t":"How a transformer uses attention","x":"Module 1 tells you that most LLMs use a transformer. This section tells you how a transformer works. The meaning of a word depends on the other words in the text. Look at the sentence \"The animal did not cross the street because it was tired\". Here, the word \"it\" refers to the animal. In \"... because it was too wide\", the word \"it\" refers to the street. A method with the name attention solves this problem. For each token, attention calculates how important each other token is. Then it adds information from the important tokens into this token. Refer to Figure 6. Figure 6. Attention. The model learns that \"it\" refers to \"animal\". A real model calculates this for each token and in many layers. The numbers are only an example. The steps in a transformer The system cuts the text into tokens. The system changes each token into an embedding. It also adds information about the position of each token. In an attention layer, each token collects information from the other tokens. In a feedforward layer, each token goes through a small neural network. The model does steps 3 and 4 again in many layers. A large model can have more than 100 layers. The last layer gives the probability of each possible next token. Refer to Module 1, Section 6 . A transformer does the calculations for all tokens at the same time. Thus it can use the power of GPUs well. This is one reason why transformers replaced the older designs. Play video: Attention in transformers, step-by-step (3Blue1Brown) Video 3. Attention in transformers, step by step (3Blue1Brown) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"deep-learning-in-practice","n":"9","t":"Deep learning in practice","x":"Computer power Deep learning needs very many calculations. A GPU is a computer chip that does many calculations at the same time. Small networks can train on one GPU. Large language models train on thousands of GPUs for weeks or months. Prevent overfitting A deep network has very many weights. Thus it can easily remember the training data. Refer to Module 2, Section 9 . Use these methods to prevent overfitting: Dropout . During training, the system switches off some random neurons. Thus the network cannot depend on a small number of neurons. Data augmentation . The system makes more training examples from the existing examples. For example, it turns, flips, or crops each image. Early stopping. The system stops training when the validation loss starts to increase. Transfer learning To train a large network from zero, you need very much data and money. Transfer learning is a different method: Start with a network that is already trained on a large data set. For example, a network that is trained on millions of photographs. Train it a little more on your small data set. For example, 500 photographs of defects on your parts. The network keeps its general knowledge, for example how to find edges and shapes. It only learns the new task. Generative AI uses the same principle. Refer to Module 4, Section 4 . Warning It is difficult to know why a deep network gives an answer. Do not use a deep network alone for decisions that have a large effect on people, for example a loan or a medical diagnosis. A qualified person must do a check of the decision."},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"summary","n":"10","t":"Summary","x":"Deep learning uses neural networks with many layers. The network finds the features in the raw data. Each neuron multiplies its inputs by weights, adds a bias, and applies an activation function. Backpropagation finds the gradient for each weight. Gradient descent then adjusts the weights. CNNs use filters to find patterns in images. Embeddings change items into lists of numbers. Similar items have similar embeddings. Transformers use attention to connect each token to the other tokens. Transfer learning gives good results with less data."},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":4,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"This module gives a description of generative AI. It tells you: What generative AI is, and how it is different from predictive AI. The types of content that generative AI can make. How a large language model is made. How a model selects its output, and how you can control it. How an image generator makes a picture. How to write a good prompt. How to connect a model to your documents and to tools. The risks of generative AI, and how to use it responsibly."},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":4,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"what-generative-ai-is","n":"2","t":"What generative AI is","x":"Most models in Module 2 are predictive. A predictive model looks at an input. Then it gives a label or a number. For example, it tells you if an email is spam. Generative AI is different. It makes new content. This content did not exist before. The model learns the patterns in its training data. Then it uses these patterns to make new examples that are similar to the data. Refer to Figure 1. Figure 1. A predictive model selects an answer. A generative model makes new content from a description. A large generative model can do many different tasks. We call this type of model a foundation model . Companies use one foundation model in many products, for example chat, writing help, code help, and search. Note Generative AI does not copy text or pictures from a database. It makes new output from the patterns that it learned. But sometimes the output can be very similar to an item in the training data."},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":4,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"types-of-generative-ai","n":"3","t":"Types of generative AI","x":"Generative models can make different types of content. We call each type of content a modality . Output Examples of tasks Usual type of model Text Answer questions, write drafts, make summaries, and translate. Large language model (LLM) Code Write, explain, and repair program code. LLM that is also trained on code Images Make a picture from a text description. Change a part of a photograph. Diffusion model. Refer to Section 6. Sound and speech Change text into speech. Make music. Make a copy of a voice. Transformer or diffusion model Video Make short video clips from a text or from an image. Diffusion model 3D and design Make 3D models, product designs, and game content. Diffusion model or transformer A multimodal model can use more than one modality. For example, it can receive a photograph and a question. Then it writes an answer about the photograph."},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":4,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"how-a-large-language-model-is-made","n":"4","t":"How a large language model is made","x":"A modern chat AI is made in three stages. Refer to Figure 2. Figure 2. The three stages that make a chat AI. Stage 1 uses the most data and the most computer time. Stage 1: Pretraining In pretraining , the model reads a very large quantity of text. Its task is to predict the next token. For more information about tokens, refer to Module 1, Section 6 . Most of these models are transformers. Refer to Module 3, Section 8 . Pretraining can take months. It uses thousands of special computer chips at the same time. We call these chips GPUs . Pretraining is the most expensive stage. The result is a base model . A base model knows much about language and about the world. But it only continues text. If you ask it a question, it can write more questions in place of an answer. Stage 2: Fine-tuning In fine-tuning , the model learns from a smaller set of examples. Each example has a question and a good answer. People write or check these examples. After fine-tuning, the model follows instructions. Fine-tuning is a type of transfer learning. Refer to Module 3, Section 9 . Stage 3: Human feedback The model writes two or more answers to the same question. People select the better answer. The model learns to give the type of answer that people prefer. This method uses reinforcement learning. Refer to Module 2, Section 7 . We call this method RLHF . After these three stages, the model is an assistant. It follows instructions, and it refuses some harmful requests. Note A model does not learn from your conversation immediately. Its weights change only when the company trains it again. But some companies use conversations as training data. Read the privacy settings of each AI tool that you use. Play video: Intro to Large Language Models (Andrej Karpathy) Video 1. Intro to large language models (Andrej Karpathy, approx. 1 h) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":4,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"how-a-model-selects-its-output","n":"5","t":"How a model selects its output","x":"Module 1 tells you that a model calculates a probability for each possible next token. Then the system selects one token. We call this selection sampling . If the system always selects the token with the highest probability, the text is predictable. But the text can become repetitive. If the system sometimes selects a token with a lower probability, the text is more varied. A setting with the name temperature controls this. Refer to Figure 3. Figure 3. At a low temperature, the most probable token is almost always selected. At a high temperature, other tokens are selected more frequently. The numbers are only an example. Temperature Effect Use it for Low (0 to 0.3) The answer is almost the same each time. Facts, calculations, code, and data extraction. Medium (0.5 to 0.8) The answer changes a little each time. Usual chat and writing. High (1.0 or more) The answer changes much each time. Errors are more probable. Ideas, poems, and stories. Note Because of sampling, the same prompt can give a different answer each time. This is usual. It is not a fault. The context window A model can use only a limited quantity of text at one time. We call this limit the context window . The context window includes your prompt, the documents that you give, the conversation until now, and the answer. The size of a context window is given in tokens. In English, 1,000 tokens are approximately 750 words. If a conversation is longer than the context window, the model cannot use the earliest parts."},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":4,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"how-an-image-generator-works","n":"6","t":"How an image generator works","x":"Most image generators use a diffusion model . A diffusion model learns to remove noise from an image. Refer to Figure 4. Figure 4. A diffusion model. In training, the system adds noise to images. In generation, the model starts from noise and removes it. The prompt guides each step. A real model uses 20 to 50 steps. Training The system takes an image and its text description from the training data. The system adds a small quantity of random noise to the image. The model tries to predict the noise that the system added. The system compares the prediction with the real noise. Then it adjusts the weights. The system repeats these steps with more noise, millions of times. Generation You write a prompt. The system starts with an image that is only random noise. The model removes a small quantity of noise. The prompt tells the model what to find in the noise. The system repeats step 3 many times. A clear image appears. It is a new image that matches the prompt. Video generators use the same principle. They also keep the frames of the video consistent with each other. Play video: But how do AI images and videos actually work? (Welch Labs, on 3Blue1Brown) Video 2. But how do AI images and videos actually work? (Welch Labs, on 3Blue1Brown) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":4,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"how-to-write-a-good-prompt","n":"7","t":"How to write a good prompt","x":"The quality of the answer depends on the quality of the prompt. A good prompt gives the model the information that it needs. A good prompt can have five parts. Refer to Figure 5. Figure 5. The five parts of a good prompt. The task is always necessary. Add the other parts when they help. These examples show how to make a weak prompt better: Weak prompt Better prompt Write about rainbows. Explain how a rainbow forms, for students who are 10 years old. Use 5 short steps. Fix my code. This Python function must return the sum of a list. It gives an error when the list is empty. Find the cause and repair it. The code is below. Summarize this. Make a summary of this report for a manager. Use 5 bullet points. Include all costs and dates. Useful methods Give examples. Show one to three examples of the result that you want. We call this few-shot prompting . Ask for steps. For a problem with calculations or logic, ask the model to show its steps. Some new models do this automatically before they answer. We call them reasoning models . Divide a large task. Give the model one part of the task at a time. Let the model say \"I do not know\". Tell the model to say this when it does not have the information. This can decrease hallucinations. Try again. If the answer is not good, change the prompt. Tell the model what is wrong. Many AI products add hidden instructions before your prompt. We call these instructions the system prompt . The system prompt gives the model its role and its rules. Procedure: write a good prompt Write the task in one clear sentence. Give the context. Tell the model who will read the result, and why. Give the facts or the text that the model must use. Tell the model the format, the length, and the style of the answer. If possible, give one example of a good result. Send the prompt. Read the answer carefully. If the answer is not correct, change the prompt and send it again."},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":4,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"connect-a-model-to-documents-and-tools","n":"8","t":"Connect a model to documents and tools","x":"A model knows only its training data. It does not know your company documents or the news of today. A method with the name RAG solves this problem. Refer to Figure 6. Figure 6. RAG. The system finds relevant text in your documents. It adds this text to the prompt. Then the model writes an answer from this text. A user asks a question. The system searches your documents for text that is related to the question. The search usually uses embeddings . Thus it finds text with the same meaning, not only the same words. Refer to Module 3, Section 7 . The system puts the question and the found text into one prompt. The model writes an answer from this text. The answer shows the sources. You can use them to do a check of the answer. RAG has these advantages: The answers can use new and private data. You can do a check of each source. There are usually fewer hallucinations. It is not necessary to train the model again when the documents change. Tools and agents Some AI systems can use tools. For example, they can search the web, do calculations, run code, or send an email. An AI agent is a system that uses an LLM to plan steps. Then it uses tools to complete a task. Example task for an agent: \"Find three hotels in Pune for less than ₹5,000 per night. Make a table of their prices and ratings.\" Caution An agent can do actions in the real world. Make sure that a person approves each important action, for example a payment, an order, or an email. Play video: What is Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)? (IBM Technology) Video 3. What is retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)? (IBM Technology) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":4,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"risks-of-generative-ai","n":"9","t":"Risks of generative AI","x":"Warning Generative AI can make content that looks real but is false. This includes text, photographs, voices, and videos. Do not believe content only because it looks real. Know these risks: Hallucinations. A model can invent facts, quotes, and references. The text can sound correct and confident. Refer to Module 1, Section 8 . Deepfakes. A deepfake is a false image, video, or voice of a real person. Criminals use deepfakes for fraud. For example, a false voice of a family member asks for money. Copyright. Models are trained on content that people made. The rules about the use of this content are different in different countries. The output can also be similar to an existing work. Bias. Text and images from a model can repeat unfair ideas from the training data. Prompt injection. A web page or a document can contain hidden instructions for an AI. We call this prompt injection . These instructions can make an AI agent do something that you did not ask for. Private data. Data that you give to an AI tool can be kept by the company that makes the tool. Too much trust. If the AI does all the work, you learn less. Use AI to help you learn, not in place of your learning. Energy. Training and use of large models need much electricity and water for cooling. Caution Obey the rules of your school or company for the use of AI. Some rules tell you to say when AI helped you to make a document. Procedure: before you use AI-made content Do a check of all facts, numbers, names, and references in a reliable source. Make sure that the content does not copy the work of a different person. Make sure that the content does not contain private data. Read the content as your reader will read it. Remove errors and parts that are not clear. If your rules tell you to, say that AI helped to make the content. Accept responsibility for the final content. You are responsible for it, not the AI."},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":4,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"summary","n":"10","t":"Summary","x":"Generative AI makes new content: text, images, sound, video, and code. A chat AI is made in three stages: pretraining, fine-tuning, and human feedback. Temperature controls how predictable or varied the output is. The context window limits how much text a model can use at one time. An image generator starts from noise and removes it step by step. A good prompt gives the task, the context, the format, and an example. RAG lets a model use your documents and show its sources. AI-made content can be false. Always do a check before you use it."},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-accuracy","t":"Accuracy","x":"The percentage of all predictions that are correct.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-activation-function","t":"Activation function","x":"A simple function that a neuron applies to its total.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-agent","t":"Agent","x":"The program that learns and makes decisions in reinforcement learning.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-ai-agent","t":"AI agent","x":"An AI system that uses an LLM to plan steps and use tools to complete a task.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-attention","t":"Attention","x":"A method that lets each token use information from the other tokens.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-backpropagation","t":"Backpropagation","x":"A method that calculates the gradient for each weight, from the output back to the input.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-base-model","t":"Base model","x":"A model after pretraining only. It continues text but does not follow instructions well.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-batch","t":"Batch","x":"A small group of training examples for one update of the weights.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-bias","t":"Bias","x":"A number that a neuron adds to its total.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-classification","t":"Classification","x":"Supervised learning that predicts a category.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-clustering","t":"Clustering","x":"A task that puts similar examples into the same group.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-cnn","t":"CNN","x":"Convolutional neural network. It uses filters to find patterns in images.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-confusion-matrix","t":"Confusion matrix","x":"A table that compares the predictions with the correct answers.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-context-window","t":"Context window","x":"The maximum quantity of text, in tokens, that a model can use at one time.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-data","t":"Data","x":"The examples that a computer uses to learn.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-data-augmentation","t":"Data augmentation","x":"A method that makes more training examples from the existing examples.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-deep-learning","t":"Deep learning","x":"Machine learning that uses neural networks with many layers.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-deepfake","t":"Deepfake","x":"A false image, video, or voice of a real person, made by AI.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-diffusion-model","t":"Diffusion model","x":"A model that makes images when it removes noise step by step.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-dropout","t":"Dropout","x":"A method that switches off random neurons during training.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-embedding","t":"Embedding","x":"A list of numbers that shows the meaning of an item.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-epoch","t":"Epoch","x":"One complete pass through all of the training data.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-feature","t":"Feature","x":"An input value that the model uses to make a prediction.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-feature-map","t":"Feature map","x":"The grid of numbers that a filter makes.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-few-shot-prompting","t":"Few-shot prompting","x":"A prompt method that gives the model some examples of the task.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-filter","t":"Filter","x":"A small grid of weights that finds one type of pattern in an image.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-fine-tuning","t":"Fine-tuning","x":"Additional training on a smaller and more specific set of data.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-forward-pass","t":"Forward pass","x":"The calculation from the input layer to the output layer.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-foundation-model","t":"Foundation model","x":"A very large general model that people adapt for many tasks.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-gpu","t":"GPU","x":"Graphics processing unit. A chip that does many calculations at the same time.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-gradient","t":"Gradient","x":"The slope of the loss.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-gradient-descent","t":"Gradient descent","x":"A method that adjusts the weights step by step to decrease the loss.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-hallucination","t":"Hallucination","x":"An answer that sounds correct but is false or invented.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-label","t":"Label","x":"The correct answer for an example.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-learning-rate","t":"Learning rate","x":"A setting that controls the size of each step in gradient descent.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-llm-large-language-model","t":"LLM (large language model)","x":"A very large neural network that is trained on text.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-loss","t":"Loss","x":"A number that shows how wrong the model is.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-modality","t":"Modality","x":"One type of data, for example text, image, sound, or video.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-model","t":"Model","x":"The software that training makes. It contains the learned rules as numbers.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-multimodal","t":"Multimodal","x":"Able to use more than one modality.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-neural-network","t":"Neural network","x":"Software with many connected units in layers.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-noise","t":"Noise","x":"Random values that hide the content of an image.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-overfitting","t":"Overfitting","x":"The model remembers the training data and does not work well on new data.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-precision","t":"Precision","x":"Of all positive predictions, the percentage that is correct.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-pretraining","t":"Pretraining","x":"The first and largest stage of training. The model learns to predict the next token.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-prompt","t":"Prompt","x":"The text that you give to an AI system.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-prompt-injection","t":"Prompt injection","x":"An attack in which hidden text gives instructions to an AI system.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-rag","t":"RAG","x":"Retrieval-augmented generation. The system adds relevant text from documents to the prompt.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-reasoning-model","t":"Reasoning model","x":"A model that works through a problem in steps before it answers.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-recall","t":"Recall","x":"Of all real positive examples, the percentage that the model finds.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-regression","t":"Regression","x":"Supervised learning that predicts a number.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-relu","t":"ReLU","x":"An activation function that changes negative numbers to zero.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-reward","t":"Reward","x":"A number that tells an agent if an action was good or bad.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-rlhf","t":"RLHF","x":"Reinforcement learning from human feedback. The model learns the answers that people prefer.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-rnn","t":"RNN","x":"Recurrent neural network. It reads a sequence one item at a time.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-sampling","t":"Sampling","x":"The selection of the next token from the calculated probabilities.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-system-prompt","t":"System prompt","x":"Instructions from the maker of an AI product that the model gets before your prompt.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-temperature","t":"Temperature","x":"A setting that controls how random the output is.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-token","t":"Token","x":"A small part of text: a word, a part of a word, or a punctuation mark.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-training","t":"Training","x":"The procedure in which a model learns from data.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-transfer-learning","t":"Transfer learning","x":"Training that starts from a model that is already trained.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-transformer","t":"Transformer","x":"The neural network design that most LLMs use.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-underfitting","t":"Underfitting","x":"The model is too simple to find the pattern in the data.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-vector","t":"Vector","x":"An ordered list of numbers.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-weight","t":"Weight","x":"An adjustable number in a model. Training changes it.","g":1}];
